description:
AWAITING USER (fable-answers-s316v.md §6a): implement-vs-bless is the user's call per CLAUDE.md 4. Fable recommendation: BLESS as not-supported with a full not-supported.md entry (classic format-string-attack primitive; essentially unused in real code; needs an lvalue-argument convention for one conversion). Do not start either path until the user rules; io/print.t stays off the XDIFF list meanwhile.

t/io/print.t is perl 24/0 vs PCL 23/1 — ONE divergence, test 23 "printf with %n".

  my $n = "abc";
  printf "ok 22%n - not really a test; just printing\n", substr $n,1,1;
  print "not " x ($n ne "a5c") . "ok 23 - printf with %n (got $n)\n";

perl: `%n` writes the number of characters emitted so far (5, for "ok 22") into the LVALUE argument — here `substr $n,1,1` — so $n becomes "a5c", and %n itself emits nothing. Output: "ok 22 - ..." then "ok 23 - ... (got a5c)".

PCL: `%n` is not implemented anywhere (no handling in p-sprintf, cl/pcl-runtime.lisp:3409; no entry in docs/not-supported.md). It emits the LITERAL "%n", warns "Redundant argument in printf" because the arg is never consumed, and leaves $n as "abc". Test 22 passes only by luck (the runner's /^ok /mg matches the "ok 22" prefix of the malformed line "ok 22%n - ...").

Ruled out during triage: the `x` operator with a false/empty count is CORRECT (`"ab" x ""`, `x 0`, `x !1`, `x -1`, `x undef`, `x "0 but true"` all match perl), so the failure is purely the missing %n.

WHAT IMPLEMENTING WOULD NEED (why it is not a drive-by):
- the format parser must accept `n`;
- an LVALUE argument convention for that one conversion — sprintf currently receives evaluated copies, and %n must write back through the arg;
- the case in the wild is an lvalue `substr`, so it must go through the existing box-magic hook (reference_box_magic_hook — arylen / \substr / \pos / \vec), not a plain box set.

Probes: scratchpad w0/x1.pl (x operator, all match), w0/x2.pl (exact test 22/23 snippet).
